# This directory becomes the PUBLIC repository (ADR-0006), so it carries its own ignore
# rules rather than relying on the engine repo's root `.gitignore` -- that file does not
# travel through the split, and the first commit of a public repo cannot be un-made.
#
# What this prevents, concretely: `git init && git add .` in a freshly copied `client/`
# would otherwise commit `.DS_Store` (local Finder metadata), `dist/` (previously built
# wheels + sdists), `build/lib/kadari/` (a second, stale copy of every source file) and
# `kadari.egg-info/` -- permanently, on a repo whose history is public forever.
#
# `packaging_check.py::check_public_repo_hygiene` asserts every pattern below is still
# here, so deleting one fails CI rather than quietly re-opening the hole.

# Python build + bytecode
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
.eggs/

# Environments
.venv/
venv/

# Tooling caches
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# OS metadata -- leaks local directory structure and is pure noise in a diff
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Secrets. The client needs no credential to run and must never carry one (rule 7);
# this is belt-and-braces for anyone who forks it into their own environment.
.env
.env.*
*.key

# A contributor's own capture log is their production traffic. Never commit one.
*.jsonl
!kadari/sample_capture.jsonl

# `kadari bundle` writes a zip of production prompts. It is the single densest file of
# customer data this tool produces, it is binary (so the guard's textual scan cannot read
# it), and it lands wherever the user ran the command. `git add .` must never stage one.
*.zip
kadari_demo_report.html
report.html
